== The Application

#emph[Oh, how the consideration of these things should stir up the hearts of all, especially of those who have a blessing in their hand, an estate to give, to draw out their souls and reach out their hands to supply these needy servants of God!] Surely this is a time in which Christ is trying your#super[+] love to him by your charity to them. Are you not concerned to prove your love to Christ? Do you not have an opportunity before you, and a loud call from heaven, to prove it in this way? Can you not hear him who once said to #emph[Peter] (John 21:16), “Do you love Me? … Feed My sheep”? Can you not hear the same voice calling to you, #emph[Do you love Me? Feed My shepherds]? Surely you are very deaf to the voice #emph[of the providences of this day] if you do not hear this call. And will you prove that you have no love to Christ? What else are you doing, if you let his own starve when you have the means to feed them?

Beloved, it is through God’s free grace and rich bounty that you#super[+] enjoy such plenty of outward good things, while many of his ministers are in great want. What you#super[+] have, you have received from him. Is it not, then, most just and reasonable that you#super[+] should return some part of your estates to God by relieving his ministers, in testimony of your love and thankfulness to him for what you have? Has God given you#super[+] hundreds and thousands? And will you#super[+] not part with a few pounds or shillings when he calls aloud to you for them, as he does now in these days? Though the earth is his, and its fullness, yet (in a sense) he sometimes has need of your#super[+] estates. In Matt 25:35 Christ tells you#super[+] of his needs, and shows you how and when he is relieved.

If you have any spiritual wisdom to discern times and seasons, you may know that Christ now stands in need of your helping hand, now that so many of his ministers are in want for the testimony of a good conscience. And should you now deny him in his ministers, I truly believe that if Christ were now on earth, as when he first took our nature on him, and asked you for relief in his own person, you would turn a deaf ear to him.

Though for the present you#super[+] enjoy many blessings, yet how easily he can pluck them out of your mouths, and, if you will not do your duty, thrust you down from among the givers to take your place among the receivers! Certainly giving away a part is your#super[+] best way to secure the rest, and to season and sanctify it for your more comfortable use.

Luke 11:41. Our Savior says, “Give alms of such things as you#super[+] have, and you will see that everything is clean for you”; that is, lawful to be used. The metaphor is taken from the law, by which many things were counted #emph[unclean,] and so unlawful to use; they might not be touched or handled. Yes, by the apostle’s inference, “give alms of such things as you#super[+] have, and you will see that everything is clean for you,” he seems to allude to another rite of the law, about #emph[firstfruits, tithes,] and #emph[freewill offerings:] by giving these to the Lord, all the rest that the people had was secured, sanctified and made clean. So charitable men are those who may use what they have with the more freedom, quietness and comfort. Who, if he has any desire to be accepted and approved by God, would not have his person, the actions he does, and the things he possesses be clean? Whatever is unclean is odious and abominable in God’s sight. Unclean persons might not approach God’s sanctuary, nor handle sacred things. If they were not cleansed, they were to be cut off from God’s people (Num 19:20; Lev 7:20). And every unclean thing was an abomination.

This therefore must be a strong persuasion to put into practice the counsel of our Savior, by #emph[giving alms of such things as we have, so that everything may be clean,] and sanctified, #emph[for us.] What is clean you may use with God’s good leave and allowance (clean things might be eaten); and where you have God’s good leave, you may expect his blessing. It is sin to eat where God’s portion is mixed in with it.

The truth is, it is the great mistake of many to think that all their goods are their own, and that they are #emph[absolute lords] and #emph[owners] of all they possess. In fact they are #emph[stewards] rather than #emph[lords and owners] of this world’s goods; all is the Lord’s. God in his infinite wisdom has committed a greater portion of them to some than to others, yet not to be appropriated to themselves: they must give him his special part, to which his poor have as good a right as the rich have to the rest, except only that the poor may not lay hands on it themselves until you put it into their hands. But as they may not take it out #emph[of yours,] so you may not keep it out #emph[of their hands.] Therefore #emph[Solomon,] speaking of the right the poor have to a part of rich men’s estates, says, “Do not withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is within your power to act” (Prov 3:27). So relieving the poor is not only an act of #emph[mercy,] left to our choice to do or leave undone, but also, as I said before, an act of #emph[justice,] which we are bound to perform. Hence #emph[to give alms] and #emph[to do righteousness and justice] are often put in Scripture one for the other, both meaning the same thing. Therefore the #emph[Septuagint] has rendered the Hebrew word meaning #emph[righteousness] in Deuteronomy 24:13 by ἐλεημοσύνην, #emph[alms deeds.] And in Ps 112:9 it is said, “his righteousness endures forever”; that is, his #emph[alms,] or his #emph[mercifulness,] as is clear from the words before it: “He has scattered abroad his gifts to the poor; his righteousness endures forever.” By this it is implied that #emph[giving alms is one special branch of that righteousness and justice we owe to our poor neighbors.]

And do you dare still hold it in your hand? Will you wrong the #emph[Almighty] of his #emph[right,] and rob #emph[his poor] of their #emph[due?] If it is nothing to you to be unmerciful, is it nothing either to be a #emph[robber? A robber of God?] “Will a man rob God?” (Mal 3:8). Does any man dare to be so wicked? How can you not expect that he will make a #emph[forcible entry on you,] and take what is his own, and seal a #emph[writ of ejectment against you,] and turn you out of all that you have? Do not look to be long in quiet possession of what you have; but fear lest God should take your estate from you, or you from your estate, and give you your portion with #emph[Dives,] who, because he denied his #emph[crumbs] to satisfy poor #emph[Lazarus]’s hunger, was himself denied a #emph[drop of cold water] to quench his own heat and thirst (Luke 16:24). On these words St. #emph[Augustine] gives this gloss: #emph[Ideo rogans Dives non exauditur in tormentis, quia rogantem pauperem non exaudivit.] Therefore #emph[Dives] was not heard when he prayed in torments, because he did not listen to the poor man who begged of him when he was on the earth. #emph[Aug. Serm. De Divite.]

Let me add a few stirring words, to press home what has been said, by the following questions.

Quest. 1. #emph[Now at length,] what do you think of mercy and bounty to the poor?

1\. #emph[Is there not an excellence in it?] Oh, how much of God there is in it! #emph[There is faith in it.] The man who will cast his bread upon the waters, is it not a sure sign that he believes God, who has said that it will be returned to him? #emph[There is the love of God in it.] “If anyone … sees his brother in need, but shuts up his compassion from him, how can the love of God dwell in him?” And the man who opens his heart and draws out his soul to God’s hungry ones, who can deny that the love of God dwells in that man? #emph[It is the image of God’s goodness.] God is a merciful God, and his mercy is his glory; and this glory of God shines out in his merciful servants. #emph[There is self-denial in it.] Self would keep all; the flesh would hold back; it cannot spare anything for God. It is a sign that self is laid low, that the flesh is kept under, that the interest of God is the ruling interest, when self is not allowed to keep back whatever God will have. Is there so much of God in mercifulness, and is there not an excellence in it?

2\. #emph[Is there not a necessity of it?] Will the merciless ever obtain mercy? Can you hope for mercy if you will show no mercy?

Qu. 2. #emph[What do you think of yourself?] Are you merciful, or merciless? How do you find it? Is your heart open? Is your compassion open? Or is it still shut up? Do you feel that heart of yours inclined and resolved to open your hands, and to disperse, and that liberally, the good things you have received, as a good steward of the manifold gifts of God? Or do you still feel that the door is shut, that your heart is hardened against showing kindness to the Lord? Give your plain judgment: what do you think? Are you a merciful man, or merciless? And if merciless, how do you hope to obtain mercy?

#emph[Qu.] 3. What do you think of your worldly goods?

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Are they not your talents, committed to you for your Lord’s use?] Must you not give him an account of how they have been laid out? Will it be a comfortable account when you must bring in your bill: so much for your pride, so much for your appetite, or all hoarded up for your covetousness, and nothing, or only a little, for God?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Are they worth more than treasures in heaven?] Is it more to your advantage to be rich in this world’s goods than to be rich in good works? Do you judge in good earnest that laying up treasures on earth is better management than laying up in store for yourself a good foundation for the time to come?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Qu. 4. #emph[What do you think of the state of many of the servants of God in this age?] Are there none in want? None in distress? What, neither among ministers nor among Christians? Are they all provided for? Do not many of them eat the bread and drink the water of affliction? Are they not in great straits and very short of things? Only inquire after their condition, and you will find it so. Are you one of those who do not care how it goes with the poor friends of Christ? Are they not worth inquiring and looking after? Or at least, is not he worthy whose they are and whom they serve? Is not Christ worthy, for whose sake you should show kindness to his own? How will he take your neglect of them? Remember that word: “Whatever you#super[+] did not do for … these, you did not do for Me” (Matt 25:45).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Qu. 5. #emph[What will you do from now on?] What will be the outcome of these few words written for your learning? What are you resolved on: mercy, or cruelty? Consult your own heart. Do not lay this little book aside until you have come to a resolution one way or the other, either to accept or to reject this counsel, which is offered to you in the name of the Lord for your good. Oh, show yourself a man in whose soul Christ has an interest, and so great an interest that you will do anything at his word: pity at his word, relieve at his word, give, lend, feed, clothe, refresh the hearts of his needy ones. Let your soul say, Lord, at your word I will do it. Here is my bread, and my flesh, and my money; command what you will of it, I will not say no to you. You have given me a large portion, and every one of your servants whom you appoint to receive it will have his part; for your sake, Lord, they will have it. I am yours, and all that I have is yours. Show me your pleasure: how much, and how often, and where, and to whom I am to give it; and through the grace of God, there it will go.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Friend, will you say this? Will you, in the presence of the Lord, come to this gracious resolution? Or can you still find it in your heart to deny your Lord, and go on being as hard-hearted and close-handed as you used to be? Do not dare to say, Lord, my goods are my own, I owe you nothing. Dread such thoughts, that however much you have, the servants of Christ will be none the better for it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Once again consider: you are put to this choice, either to accept the counsel of Christ or to reject it. Which of the two will you do? If you do not resolve on mercy and liberality, you reject the word of the Lord; and if you reject his word, you reject the Lord, and in doing so you say, He will be no Lord of mine. But if you accept it, then go and do accordingly. Let the hunger and the thirst and the nakedness, the wants and distresses of his servants, feel in the relief they receive from you that you are a man to whom the Lord has not spoken in vain. Let them feel it now, and let them feel it constantly: this year, and the next year, and the next, as long as they have need to receive and you have it by you to give. Do this, and the Lord accept you; may he remember all your offerings and accept all your sacrifices; the Lord answer you in the day of your trouble; the name of the God of #emph[Jacob] defend you; the Lord grant you according to your own heart and fulfill all your counsel; the Lord remember you with the favor he bears to his children, and visit you with his salvation.]]
